uvm-core's C reaches the design for its HDL access through a back end it picks
by a macro naming a tool, and stops with an #error where no macro names one. A
back end reading the design through the IEEE 1800 VPI alone (LRM 36 to 38),
with no header of any one tool, is added beside them, taken from the release's
own Questa back end with the part reaching VHDL through that tool's own
interface left out, and selected by UVM_HDL_VPI. What the library does
through it is unchanged.

IEEE 1800.2 does not define these routines; uvm_hdl.svh states what each
does. On a variable or a net every back end of the release reaches the design
through the same VPI routines, whose results IEEE 1800 fixes: a read through
vpi_get_value (LRM 38.15), and a deposit, a force and a release through
vpi_put_value, which hands back the value after a release (LRM 38.34). The
Questa back end is the one whose access to SystemVerilog needs nothing beyond
those routines. Where the back ends differ it is in how a path is read: this
one reads and writes a part select such as sig[7:0] one bit at a time.

uvm-core takes contributions from Accellera members only, and its issue
tracker refuses new features, so this stays with the port.

--- a/src/dpi/uvm_hdl.c
+++ b/src/dpi/uvm_hdl.c
@@ -31,7 +31,9 @@
 
 
 // hdl vendor backends are defined for VCS,QUESTA,XCELIUM
-#if defined(VCS) || defined(VCSMX)
+#ifdef UVM_HDL_VPI
+#include "uvm_hdl_vpi.c"
+#elif defined(VCS) || defined(VCSMX)
 #include "uvm_hdl_vcs.c"
 #else
 #ifdef QUESTA
diff --git a/src/dpi/uvm_hdl_vpi.c b/src/dpi/uvm_hdl_vpi.c
new file mode 100644
